WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.439 --> 00:00:04.306
Something I think about
a lot is Tide Pods.

00:00:04.306 --> 00:00:07.430
Someone just made a joke one day,
that Tide Pods look delicious.

00:00:07.430 --> 00:00:11.183
And so people started, you know, just making
posts about this on social media.

00:00:11.183 --> 00:00:13.349
And it was just transparently hilarious.

00:00:13.349 --> 00:00:18.664
But the platform's incentives are such that
if you actually did eat a Tide pod,

00:00:18.664 --> 00:00:20.680
 you'd get a million views.

00:00:20.680 --> 00:00:27.000
And what had seemed super funny, 
all of a sudden was a public health hazard.

00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:29.745
Platforms, for I think
very understandable reasons,

00:00:29.745 --> 00:00:34.054
have a terrible time figuring out
when the joke stops being funny.

00:00:38.519 --> 00:00:41.809
"Twitter, for the very first time,
has fact-checked President Trump...."

00:00:41.809 --> 00:00:43.347
"...on voter misinformation."

00:00:43.347 --> 00:00:46.324
"That's the line Facebook, Twitter, 
and others seem to have drawn."

00:00:46.324 --> 00:00:49.557
"Trump continues to falsely insist 
 that the election was stolen."

00:00:49.557 --> 00:00:53.253
"Twitter has put up a flag more than 
 a hundred times since Election Day."

00:00:53.253 --> 00:00:56.587
"Trump urging his followers: 
Be there, will be wild."

00:00:56.587 --> 00:01:00.348
"Fight for Trump! Fight for Trump!"

00:01:00.348 --> 00:01:03.569
"Twitter permanently suspending 
 the president's personal account...."

00:01:03.569 --> 00:01:06.282
"...due to the risk of 
 further incitement of violence."

00:01:06.282 --> 00:01:07.032
"Facebook."

00:01:07.032 --> 00:01:07.610
"YouTube."

00:01:07.610 --> 00:01:08.110
"Pinterest."

00:01:08.110 --> 00:01:10.111
"Shopify and Paypal."

00:01:10.849 --> 00:01:16.396
And so the question is, what is the right moment
for the platform to intervene?

00:01:18.690 --> 00:01:23.950
I think we're in a period of rethinking what
misinformation is.

00:01:23.950 --> 00:01:29.930
I think the past few years, we had the thought
that misinformation was individual bad posts,

00:01:29.930 --> 00:01:34.063
and maybe some individual actors 
 that needed to be disciplined.

00:01:34.063 --> 00:01:36.831
But, if we could just prune that garden,

00:01:36.831 --> 00:01:40.558
the rest of our information 
 ecosystem would be okay. 

00:01:40.558 --> 00:01:43.157
Alex Jones is sort of the classic example.

00:01:43.157 --> 00:01:45.736
"Apple, Facebook, 
 Spotify, and YouTube..."

00:01:45.736 --> 00:01:49.013
"...have now removed content 
 associated with Jones and InfoWars."

00:01:49.013 --> 00:01:52.750
He does not have nearly the influence over
American life today.

00:01:52.750 --> 00:01:55.055
But that kind of 
 whack-a-mole approach

00:01:55.055 --> 00:01:58.841
is just not giving us the 
 information ecosystem that we want.

00:01:59.640 --> 00:02:04.923
This idea that the election has been stolen,
which we know to be false, 

00:02:04.923 --> 00:02:09.461
is being repeated ad nauseum
 all across the Internet,

00:02:09.461 --> 00:02:14.835
in private chats, in private messages,
 as well as in public.

00:02:14.835 --> 00:02:17.828
This is becoming the big lie.

00:02:17.828 --> 00:02:20.640
It's larger than any one user.

00:02:20.640 --> 00:02:23.480
It's larger than a thousand users.

00:02:23.480 --> 00:02:27.553
It's going to require a much more
serious and difficult approach

00:02:27.553 --> 00:02:29.881
than simply removing one account,

00:02:29.881 --> 00:02:32.914
no matter how prominent
that account might be.

00:02:32.914 --> 00:02:37.672
147 members of Congress voted to overturn
the results of the election 

00:02:37.672 --> 00:02:40.103
after the Capitol had been attacked.

00:02:40.103 --> 00:02:46.311
Are these platforms ready to deplatform 147
sitting members of Congress?

00:02:46.951 --> 00:02:49.155
Removing Trump was the easy part.

00:02:49.155 --> 00:02:51.843
He incited an attack on his own government.

00:02:51.843 --> 00:02:53.590
That is not a close call.

00:02:53.590 --> 00:02:58.416
The hard call is, you're about to have maybe
70 million Americans,

00:02:58.416 --> 00:03:00.790
or some huge percentage of that, talking,

00:03:00.790 --> 00:03:06.649
including in online spaces, about an election
being stolen that was not stolen.

00:03:06.649 --> 00:03:12.000
And that is going to have a lot of really
dangerous consequences.

00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:14.554
I don't think these platforms will succeed

00:03:14.554 --> 00:03:18.714
if they can only be defined 
by what they will not allow. 

00:03:18.714 --> 00:03:22.190
It's that — what are they replacing it with?

00:03:22.190 --> 00:03:25.630
There needs to be a positive, 
constructive counterbalance

00:03:25.630 --> 00:03:29.781
to all of the misinformation 
and conspiracy theories.

00:03:29.781 --> 00:03:34.887
What can they do to build a better media ecosystem?

00:03:34.887 --> 00:03:37.823
Because if we don't have 
a shared sense of reality,

00:03:37.823 --> 00:03:42.576
I truly do not believe we are going to have a 
liberal democracy in America very much longer.

